My published paper in the  ‘Indic Heritage and Culture’ Journal.

The topic is ‘Tracing the Antiquity of Image Worship in Hinduism.’

The concept of image worship of deities is present in Hinduism from quite early times, and the Rig Veda speaks of  images in various verses, such as, 
IV. 17, 4 where it says, ‘Indrasya karta svapastamo bhut,’ meaning the maker of Indra was a skilful workman; 

R.V., I. 32, 2 Tvastasmai vajram svaryam tataksa, meaning Tvasta made the thunderbolt for Indra, which could be thrown far; 

R.V., VII. 56, 14 Sahasriyam damyam bhagametam grhamedhiyam maruto jusadhvam, meaning O Maruts, accept the offerings being offered at the temple; 

R.V., I. 10, 1 and III. 53, where there are references to processions of images of gods; Sri Sukta khila where Sri is shown as a golden antelope adorned with festoons of gold and silver; among many other verses.

In my paper I have discussed the various contradictory opinions given by scholars on this subject, and have suggested (as my concluding opinion) that ancient India had image worship well in place in the BCE, and the concept of image worship, contrary to what many later historians would want us to believe, started early in Hinduism.

